# Astrophage KB — Cursor Rules

You have access to the `ap` CLI for Astrophage Knowledge Bases. Use it to understand architecture before reading source code.

## When to activate

- User asks about architecture, APIs, entities, or system design
- User asks "how does X work" or "explain the codebase"
- Exploring an unfamiliar repo

## Usage

```bash
ap discover              # ALWAYS run this first in any new repo
ap search <query>        # Search KB for anything
ap read summaries/api-spec.md    # Read specific KB file
ap read index.md         # High-level architecture overview
ap list                  # Browse all KBs on this Astrophage instance
ap fetch <kb-name>       # Pull a specific KB by name
```

## KB file locations after discover

Local cache: `~/.astrophage/<kb-name>/`

Key files to check:
- `index.md` — architecture overview and file map
- `summaries/api-spec.md` — REST APIs, endpoints, auth
- `summaries/db-schema.md` — database tables and relationships
- `concepts/business-logic.md` — domain workflows
- `decisions/adr-*.md` — why decisions were made

## Cross-KB links

If you see `ap:other-app/page` or `[[kb:other-app/page]]` in a KB file:

You can and should traverse between multiple KBs to understand information and links using the ap commands.
```bash
ap links <file>          # auto-fetch linked KB and resolve paths
ap fetch other-app       # or explicitly fetch the linked KB
```

## No KB found?

```bash
If ap discover fails, review the printed list of available KBs and explicitly run `ap link <kb_repo_url>` or `ap fetch <kb-name>` with the relevant one.
# Onboard this repo: https://astrophage.app/onboard
```
